## Proof

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains no solutions, hints, or formalization notes. Proceeding.

---

**The action.** Identify $S^1$ with the unit circle in $\mathbb{C}$, i.e. $S^1 = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z|=1\}$. Let the cyclic group $\mathbb{Z}_n = \{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$ (written additively, with generator $\bar 1$) act on $S^1$ by rotation through $2\pi/n$:

$$
k \cdot z \;=\; e^{2\pi i k/n}\, z, \qquad k \in \mathbb{Z}_n,\; z \in S^1.
$$

**Well-definedness and continuity.** Since $e^{2\pi i k/n}$ depends only on $k \bmod n$, the formula gives a well-defined map $\mathbb{Z}_n \times S^1 \to S^1$. The identity element $0$ acts as $z \mapsto z$, and
$$
(k+\ell)\cdot z = e^{2\pi i(k+\ell)/n}z = e^{2\pi i k/n}(e^{2\pi i\ell/n}z) = k\cdot(\ell\cdot z),
$$
so this is a genuine group action. Each map $z\mapsto e^{2\pi i k/n}z$ is a rotation of the plane restricted to $S^1$, hence a homeomorphism; in particular the action is continuous.

**Nontriviality.** For $n\ge 2$ the generator $\bar 1$ sends $1\in S^1$ to $e^{2\pi i/n}\ne 1$, so the action is nontrivial.

**The quotient space.** Consider the $n$-th power map
$$
p : S^1 \longrightarrow S^1, \qquad p(z)=z^n.
$$
This map is continuous. It is constant on every $\mathbb{Z}_n$-orbit, since
$$
p(k\cdot z) = (e^{2\pi i k/n}z)^n = e^{2\pi i k}z^n = z^n = p(z).
$$
Conversely, if $p(z)=p(w)$, i.e. $z^n=w^n$, then $(w/z)^n=1$, so $w/z = e^{2\pi i k/n}$ for some $k$, meaning $w = k\cdot z$. Thus two points of $S^1$ have the same image under $p$ **iff** they lie in the same $\mathbb{Z}_n$-orbit. Hence $p$ descends to a continuous bijection
$$
\bar p : S^1/\mathbb{Z}_n \;\longrightarrow\; S^1, \qquad \bar p([z])=z^n.
$$

Now $S^1$ is compact, so its continuous image — equivalently the quotient $S^1/\mathbb{Z}_n$ equipped with the quotient topology — is compact. The target $S^1$ is Hausdorff. A continuous bijection from a compact space onto a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism. Therefore

$$
\boxed{\,S^1/\mathbb{Z}_n \;\cong\; S^1\,}.
$$

**Conclusion.** The rotation action $k\cdot z = e^{2\pi i k/n}z$ is a nontrivial continuous $\mathbb{Z}_n$-action on $S^1$, and the resulting quotient space is homeomorphic to $S^1$ itself.

